.TH "LLVM-IFS" "1" "2026-07-15" "22" "LLVM"
.SH "نام (NAME)"
llvm-ifs \- shared object stubbing tool
.SH "خلاصه دستور (SYNOPSIS)"
.sp
\fBllvm\-ifs\fP [\fIoptions\fP] \fIinputs\fP
.SH "توضیحات (DESCRIPTION)"
.sp
\fBllvm\-ifs\fP is a tool that jointly produces human\-readable text\-based
stubs (.ifs files) for shared objects and linkable shared object stubs
(.so files) from either ELF shared objects or text\-based stubs. The text\-based
stubs is useful for monitoring ABI changes of the shared object. The linkable
shared object stubs can be used to avoid unnecessary relinks when the ABI of
shared libraries does not change.
.SH IFS FORMATS
.sp
Here is an example of the text representation (IFS) of a shared object produced
by the \fBllvm\-ifs\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\-\-\- !ifs\-v1
IFSVersion: 3.0
SoName: libtest.so /* Optional */
Target: x86_64\-unknown\-linux\-gnu   /* Optional, format 1, same format as llvm target triple */
Target: { Arch: x86_64, Endianness: little, Bitwidth: 64 } /* Optional, format 2 */
NeededLibs:
  \- libc.so.6
Symbols:
  \- { Name: sym0, Type: Notype }
  \- { Name: sym1, Type: Object, Size: 0 }
  \- { Name: sym2, Type: Func, Weak: false }
  \- { Name: sym3, Type: TLS }
  \- { Name: sym4, Type: Unknown, Warning: foo }
\&...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIFSVersion\fP: Version of the IFS file for reader compatibility.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSoName\fP (optional): Name of the shared object file that is being stubbed.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTarget\fP (optional): The architecture, endianness and bitwise information of
this shared object. It can be either in explicit format or in implicit LLVM
triple format. It can be optional and can be overridden from command line
options.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNeededLibs\fP: The list of the external shared objects that this library depends on.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSymbols\fP: A collection of all data needed to link objects for each symbol, sorted by name in ascending order.
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBName\fP: Symbol name.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBType\fP: Whether the symbol is an object, function, no\-type, thread local storage, or unknown. Symbol types not explicitly supported are mapped as unknown to improve signal\-to\-noise ratio.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSize\fP: The size of the symbol in question, doesn\(aqt apply to functions, and is optional for NoType symbols.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUndefined\fP: Whether or not the symbol is defined in this shared object file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWeak\fP: Whether or not the symbol should be treated as weak.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWarning\fP (optional): Warning text to output when this symbol is linked against.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This YAML based text format contains everything that is needed to generate a
linkable ELF shared object as well as an Apple TAPI format file. The ordering
of symbols is sorted, so these files can be easily compared using diff tools.
If the content of the file changes, it indicates a potentially ABI breaking
change.
.SH ELF STUB FORMAT
.sp
A minimum ELF file that can be used by linker should have following sections properly populated:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
ELF header.
.IP \(bu 2
Section headers.
.IP \(bu 2
Dynamic symbol table (\fB\&.dynsym\fP section).
.IP \(bu 2
Dynamic string table (\fB\&.dynstr\fP section).
.IP \(bu 2
Dynamic table (\fB\&.dynamic\fP section).
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDT_SYMTAB\fP entry.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDT_STRTAB\fP entry.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDT_STRSZ\fP entry.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDT_NEEDED\fP entries. (optional)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDT_SONAME\fP entry. (optional)
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
Section header string table (\fB\&.shstrtab\fP section)
.UNINDENT
.sp
This ELF file may have compatibility issues with ELF analysis tools that rely on the program headers.
Linkers like LLD work fine with such a minimum ELF file without errors.
.SH "گزینه‌ها (OPTIONS)"
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-input\-format=[IFS|ELF|OtherObjectFileFormats]
Specify input file format. Currently, only text IFS files and ELF shared
object files are supported. This flag is optional as the input format can be
inferred.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-output\-elf=<output\-filename>
Specify the output file for ELF shared object stub.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-output\-ifs=<output\-filename>
Specify the output file for text IFS.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-output\-tbd=<output\-filename>
Specify the output file for Apple TAPI tbd.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-arch=[x86_64|AArch64|...]
This flag is optional and it should only be used when reading an IFS file
which does not define the \fBArch\fP (architecture). This flag defines the
architecture of the output file, and can be any string supported by ELF
\(aqe_machine\(aq field. If the value is conflicting with the IFS file, an error
will be reported and the program will stop.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-endianness=[little|big]
This flag is optional and it should only be used when reading an IFS file
which does not define the \fBEndianness\fP\&. This flag defines the endianness of
the output file. If the value is conflicting with the IFS file, an error
will be reported and the program will stop.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-bitwidth=[32|64]
This flag is optional and it should only be used when reading an IFS file
which does not define the \fBBitWidth\fP\&. This flag defines the bit width of the
output file. If the value is conflicting with the input IFS file, an error
will be reported and the program will stop.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-target=<target triple>
This flag is optional and should only be used when reading an IFS file
which does not define any target information. This flag defines architecture,
endianness and bit width of the output file using llvm target triple.
This flag cannot be used simultaneously with other target related flags.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-hint\-ifs\-target=<target triple>
This flag is optional and should only be used when reading an ELF shared
object and generating an IFS file. by default, llvm\-ifs will use \(aq\fBArch\fP,
\fBEndianness\fP and \fBBitWidth\fP\(aq fields to reflect the target information from the
input object file. Using this flag will tell llvm\-ifs the expected target
triple in the output IFS file. If the value matches the target information
from the object file, this value will be used in the \(aqTarget:\(aq filed in the
generated IFS. If it conflicts with the input object file, an error will be
reported and the program will stop.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-hint\-ifs\-target
This flag is optional and should only be used when outputting an IFS file.
This flag strips the \fBArch\fP field from the IFS file so it can be overridden
later.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-strip\-ifs\-endianness
This flag is optional and should only be used when outputting an IFS file.
This flag strips the \fBEndianness\fP field from the IFS file so it can be
overridden later.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-strip\-ifs\-bitwidth
This flag is optional and should only be used when outputting an IFS file.
This flag strips the \fBBitWidth\fP field from the IFS file so it can be overridden
later.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-strip\-ifs\-target
This flag is optional and should only be used when outputting an IFS file.
This flag strips the \fBTarget\fP field from the IFS file so it can be overridden
later.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-write\-if\-changed
When this flag is set, llvm\-ifs will only write the output file if it does not
already exist or the content will be different from the existing file.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-strip\-size
When this flag is set, llvm\-ifs will remove the size field from the output ifs
file. This is useful for shared objects that only intend to be linked against
position independent code which doesn\(aqt need copy relocations, or where the size
of an object is not a useful part of the abi to track.
.UNINDENT
.SH "وضعیت خروج (EXIT STATUS)"
.sp
If \fBllvm\-ifs\fP succeeds, it will exit with 0. Otherwise, if an
error occurs, it will exit with a non\-zero value.
.SH "نویسنده (AUTHOR)"
